1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,073
Multi-agent systems usually fail for a
reason that has nothing to do with the

2
00:00:03,127 --> 00:00:05,313
model. They fail because
the organization is wrong.

3
00:00:05,367 --> 00:00:08,441
Every framework hands you one architecture,
and every problem gets bent to fit

4
00:00:08,494 --> 00:00:10,307
it. But work has a shape of its own.

5
00:00:10,521 --> 00:00:12,014
Graph engineering designs that shape.

6
00:00:13,184 --> 00:00:14,731
So treat the organization as a graph.

7
00:00:15,051 --> 00:00:18,837
Agents and humans are nodes. Delegation,
review and escalation are typed edges.

8
00:00:19,051 --> 00:00:20,544
What you engineer is the topology.

9
00:00:20,757 --> 00:00:24,070
And the absence of an edge is a
design decision, not an omission —

10
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:26,123
what is disconnected carries as
much intent as what is connected.

11
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,593
Every topology answers
the same ten questions.

12
00:00:29,647 --> 00:00:31,679
Who does the work. Who can talk to whom.

13
00:00:31,733 --> 00:00:34,133
Who can command whom. Where
iteration is allowed.

14
00:00:34,239 --> 00:00:36,799
Who approves progression.
Where a human is required.

15
00:00:36,906 --> 00:00:37,919
And when the work is finished.

16
00:00:39,203 --> 00:00:42,296
The paper maps fifteen patterns,
one per organizational problem.

17
00:00:42,456 --> 00:00:44,083
The patterns are not inventions —

18
00:00:44,136 --> 00:00:47,535
directed workflows, actor systems and
supervisor trees predate this by decades.

19
00:00:47,749 --> 00:00:48,922
The contribution is the mapping:

20
00:00:48,975 --> 00:00:52,015
which shape answers which problem, and that
choosing it belongs to the organization,

21
00:00:52,069 --> 00:00:52,869
not the vendor.

22
00:00:53,719 --> 00:00:56,705
Two of them, side by side. Pattern
six is a hypothesis swarm:

23
00:00:56,759 --> 00:00:59,372
several agents, each assigned a different
explanation, feeding evidence to a

24
00:00:59,425 --> 00:01:00,865
verifier whose job is to falsify.

25
00:01:00,919 --> 00:01:03,782
Look at what is missing. There is
no edge between the investigators —

26
00:01:03,835 --> 00:01:06,155
because two agents that can talk
can converge on each other's error.

27
00:01:06,315 --> 00:01:08,022
Now pattern five, incident command.

28
00:01:08,129 --> 00:01:11,062
Same blocks, and every edge runs both ways,
because a commander needs one coherent

29
00:01:11,115 --> 00:01:13,485
picture in real time. More
communication is not better.

30
00:01:13,645 --> 00:01:14,605
Correct communication is.

31
00:01:15,915 --> 00:01:17,568
Pattern ten is the one that scales.

32
00:01:17,728 --> 00:01:21,697
A chief executive agent, a coordinator,
fleet managers under that, bounded worker

33
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:24,043
fleets under them, reports
compressed on the way up.

34
00:01:24,203 --> 00:01:26,390
The chief executive does not
communicate with fifty workers.

35
00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:28,363
That is coordination cost, compressed.

36
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:33,220
Pattern ten, instantiated. Four Claude
sessions, each in its own pane, and a

37
00:01:33,273 --> 00:01:34,873
fifth pane that is the
board they report to.

38
00:01:35,087 --> 00:01:38,500
Watch the shape of it. The chief executive
posts the aim, then sends exactly

39
00:01:38,553 --> 00:01:41,183
one message — down, to
its one direct report.

40
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:43,690
The fleet manager splits the
work and sends two orders down.

41
00:01:43,903 --> 00:01:46,570
The workers never talk to each other,
because they have no edge to talk over.

42
00:01:46,730 --> 00:01:48,063
And nothing reports back by message:

43
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:51,055
every result goes onto the board, because
a message can be missed and a post

44
00:01:51,109 --> 00:01:53,722
cannot. That is about a minute
of real time, sped up here.

45
00:01:54,845 --> 00:01:56,712
Here is that same run as
the board recorded it.

46
00:01:56,765 --> 00:01:58,472
One line per post, seconds from the first.

47
00:01:58,525 --> 00:01:59,965
The chief executive opens the board.

48
00:02:00,019 --> 00:02:01,725
Within ten seconds all
three have checked in.

49
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:03,219
The order goes down one tier, then two.

50
00:02:03,522 --> 00:02:06,269
Both workers report verified within
a few seconds of each other —

51
00:02:06,322 --> 00:02:08,535
independently, which is the whole
point of giving them no edge.

52
00:02:08,695 --> 00:02:11,095
The manager certifies, and only then
does the chief executive close it.

53
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:14,241
About a minute, end to end. And what
those two workers checked were two claims

54
00:02:14,294 --> 00:02:17,441
this video makes: that the cap is three, and
that the catalogue has fifteen patterns.

55
00:02:18,711 --> 00:02:21,164
So build that shape against
rysh's own roadmap.

56
00:02:21,271 --> 00:02:22,764
Five epics become five units.

57
00:02:22,871 --> 00:02:26,497
The template lays a roadmap manager over
a chief executive, a fleet manager per

58
00:02:26,551 --> 00:02:28,086
unit, three workers under each.

59
00:02:28,406 --> 00:02:29,899
Twenty-two agents, four tiers.

60
00:02:30,113 --> 00:02:33,419
And this is a dry run — the count arrives
before anything opens, and before anything

61
00:02:33,473 --> 00:02:34,273
is spent.

62
00:02:34,896 --> 00:02:36,389
Now scale out instead of down.

63
00:02:36,656 --> 00:02:39,909
One fleet per epic, twenty-two of them,
registered against a live session.

64
00:02:40,123 --> 00:02:43,043
Every one of those registrations opened
exactly nothing, and the command says

65
00:02:43,097 --> 00:02:45,443
so itself. A registered fleet is metadata:

66
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:48,003
a name, a state, a board, a member list.

67
00:02:49,113 --> 00:02:50,713
Because running is the
part that is bounded.

68
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:52,633
The registry caps
concurrent fleets at three.

69
00:02:52,687 --> 00:02:53,860
Promote three and they go up.

70
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:55,860
Ask for a fourth and it is refused —

71
00:02:55,913 --> 00:02:58,297
and the refusal names the three already
running, instead of queueing quietly

72
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:00,323
and looking like success. Two precisions.

73
00:03:00,537 --> 00:03:02,777
This recording moves registry
state and launches no agents.

74
00:03:02,937 --> 00:03:05,923
The measured run did: on the eleventh of
August, twenty twenty-six, twenty-two

75
00:03:05,977 --> 00:03:08,813
fleets registered, three
brought up, four agents each —

76
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:12,067
twelve live Claude sessions, twelve
git worktrees, zero failures —

77
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:13,533
and nineteen refused by this same cap.

78
00:03:13,587 --> 00:03:15,987
And the cap is a constant in the
registry, not a rule in the driver.

79
00:03:17,192 --> 00:03:20,179
That is the general move, and it is why
topology deserves the same review rigour

80
00:03:20,232 --> 00:03:23,432
as code. Structure gives guarantees that
hold even when an agent misbehaves.

81
00:03:23,539 --> 00:03:26,566
An agent with no edge to production cannot
deploy, however convinced it is that

82
00:03:26,619 --> 00:03:29,499
it should. Parallel nodes with no lateral
edge cannot contaminate each other's

83
00:03:29,553 --> 00:03:31,526
thinking. None of it depends
on a model behaving well.

84
00:03:32,743 --> 00:03:34,556
And now the part that
usually goes missing.

85
00:03:34,663 --> 00:03:37,356
Rysh's worktree isolation is
file isolation on your machine —

86
00:03:37,409 --> 00:03:39,943
not containers, not sandboxes,
not remote execution.

87
00:03:40,049 --> 00:03:42,236
The board is a monitoring
view, not an audit log.

88
00:03:42,503 --> 00:03:45,613
The chart arranging agents into a chief
executive, managers and workers is in-house

89
00:03:45,666 --> 00:03:48,013
tooling on shipped primitives,
not a shipped orchestrator.

90
00:03:48,279 --> 00:03:50,839
The registry and the fleet commands you
just watched are merged on the development

91
00:03:50,893 --> 00:03:52,556
branch, not in a numbered release.

92
00:03:52,716 --> 00:03:55,223
And we do not claim fully
autonomous multi-agent delivery.

93
00:03:55,436 --> 00:03:58,023
These patterns make an agent
organization governable —

94
00:03:58,076 --> 00:03:59,169
a different promise from unattended.

